\begin_layout Title
Quiz 1: KKT Regimes in a Quantity-Quality Household
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
ECON 803 - Microeconomic Theory I - Fall 2026
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
October 2, 2026 - 25 minutes - 20 points
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Name: ............................................................ Student number: ........................................
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Show the KKT conditions and enough algebra to identify every active set.
 Children are divisible in this classroom model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
From Galor's mechanism to three static snapshots
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In Galor's Unified Growth Theory,
 the time cost of 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 children with education 
\begin_inset Formula $e$
\end_inset

 is proportional to 
\begin_inset Formula $n(\tau+e)$
\end_inset

.
 A higher return to education moves resources from child quantity toward child quality.
 Galor's model is dynamic and maintains positive fertility.
 To study three KKT active sets in one class,
 we use the following static teaching extension.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $H\geq0$
\end_inset

 be total purchased human capital for all children.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $n>0$
\end_inset

,
 quality per child is 
\begin_inset Formula $q=H/n$
\end_inset

.
 Each regime also specifies a small fertility floor 
\begin_inset Formula $\underline n_r\geq0$
\end_inset

.
 The household chooses 
\begin_inset Formula $c,H\geq0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $n\geq\underline n_r$
\end_inset

 to maximize
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula
\[
U(c,n,H)=c+6n-\frac12n^2+4H-\frac12H^2
\]
\end_inset

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
subject to the regime-specific budget and quality cone
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula
\[
c+pn+kH+\frac12H^2\leq20,
\qquad
sn\leq H\leq4n,
\qquad
n\geq\underline n_r.
\]
\end_inset

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The term 
\begin_inset Formula $H^2/2$
\end_inset

 makes total education expenditure convex.
 The cone makes 
\begin_inset Formula $q\in[s,4]$
\end_inset

 when children are present and forces 
\begin_inset Formula $H=0$
\end_inset

 when a regime permits 
\begin_inset Formula $n=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
1. General KKT system [8 points]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(a) Use multipliers 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda,\mu_c,\mu_n,\alpha,\beta\geq0$
\end_inset

 for,
 respectively,
 the budget slack,
 
\begin_inset Formula $c\geq0$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $n-\underline n_r\geq0$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $H-sn\geq0$
\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $4n-H\geq0$
\end_inset

.
 Write the Lagrangian and the complete KKT system.
 [6]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(b) Explain why these conditions are sufficient.
 Show that whenever 
\begin_inset Formula $c^*>0$
\end_inset

,
 the budget binds and 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda^*=1$
\end_inset

.
 [2]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
2. Solve three regimes [9 points]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For each regime,
 find 
\begin_inset Formula $(c^*,n^*,H^*)$
\end_inset

,
 report 
\begin_inset Formula $q^*=H^*/n^*$
\end_inset

 when it is defined,
 identify the active inequalities,
 and give one valid multiplier vector.
 You may first use the binding budget to eliminate consumption.
 [3 points each]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
Malthusian: 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,k,s,\underline n_r)=(2,5,0,0)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
Modern quantity-quality balance: 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,k,s,\underline n_r)=(4,2,0,0)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
High-quality-cost,
 low-fertility (post-modern) extension: 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,k,s,\underline n_r)=(4,6,2,1/4)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
3. Interpretation [3 points]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(a) Describe the movement from quantity to quality across your first two solutions.
 [1]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(b) Explain why a high quality price 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 alone need not eliminate fertility,
 but a high 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 together with the minimum-quality requirement 
\begin_inset Formula $H\geq2n$
\end_inset

 can push fertility down to its small lower bound.
 [1]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(c) State one dynamic feedback in Unified Growth Theory that this quiz holds fixed.
 Also state why the binding low-fertility floor should be called our extension rather than Galor's prediction.
 [1]
\end_layout

